# tools/crypto_kumara_pq_identity_witness.rish — the POST-QUANTUM identity FRONT DOOR: one backup phrase grown into the TWO lattice keys a
# quantum-resistant identity must carry (an ML-DSA signing key AND an ML-KEM key-agreement key), authored in pure Rye. The post-quantum sibling of
# crypto/kumara_identity.rye, double-seated beside it — the classical Ed25519 identity stays exactly what it is. It composes only GREEN rungs
# (crypto/mldsa_identity.rye the signing half, crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye the agreement half, crypto/bip39_seed.rye the mnemonic→seed fold, and
# crypto/hkdf_sha512.rye the RFC 5869 domain-separated key expansion), authoring no new cryptography (Season G).
#
# Season: crypto — the construction of the learned discovery that a post-quantum identity is DUAL-KEY, because the lattice schemes share no curve for
# one key to sign and agree the way Ed25519's edwards and montgomery halves do (active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-192400_post-quantum-identity-is-dual-key.md).
# One HKDF extract over the 512-bit BIP-39 seed, three domain-separated expansions carve the ML-DSA seed ξ and the ML-KEM seeds d and z — so a keeper
# still remembers ONE phrase yet holds two independent keys. The door holds no key: every mnemonic, seed, and identity is a public test vector.
# Clean-room: BIP-39, RFC 5869, FIPS 203/204 are public standards studied through their specs; the composition is our own Rye wiring over our own GREEN
# rungs, no line copied (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/kumara_pq_identity.rye is proven at every tier (small ML-DSA-44/ML-KEM-512, default ML-DSA-65/ML-KEM-768, large ML-DSA-87/ML-KEM-1024):
# the BIP-39 half anchored to a REAL published answer (the Trezor vector seed byte-for-byte), NO DRIFT (from_mnemonic equals hand-deriving the three
# seeds then composing the halves, from_seed reaching the identical identity), THE TWO KEYS DISTINCT (ξ, d, z pairwise different — the dual-key point),
# a REAL WORKING SIGNING HALF (verify accepts a genuine signature, refuses a flipped message, a flipped signature, and a wrong key), a SEALING-READY
# AGREEMENT HALF (a message sealed to the ML-KEM half opens with its dk, a different identity fails OpenFailed), BOTH HALVES AT ONCE (a frame sealed
# for confidentiality and signed for provenance, a tamper breaking the signature), plus determinism and distinctness. Each half is doubly-oracle-proven
# one rung down, so this composition needs no new oracle. Purely local — no key held, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_kumara_pq_identity_witness.rish

say "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: Language EN — the post-quantum identity front door: one backup phrase grown into the two lattice keys a quantum-resistant identity must carry, an ML-DSA signing key and an ML-KEM key-agreement key, because no single lattice key signs and agrees the way one Ed25519 key does. One HKDF extract over the 512-bit BIP-39 seed keys a single pseudorandom key; three domain-separated expansions carve the ML-DSA seed and the two ML-KEM seeds, so a keeper remembers one phrase yet holds two independent keys. The post-quantum sibling of crypto/kumara_identity.rye, double-seated beside it, composing only GREEN rungs."
say "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as one name guarded from both directions at once: the forger held off by the signing half, the eavesdropper by the agreement half, and the phrase a keeper remembers still only one. The two keys are provably distinct key material drawn from disjoint expansions of a single secret; the identity plugs straight into the post-quantum sealed door, delivering confidentiality and provenance together — what one lattice key cannot."
say "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: Lens TAME — contract asserts on every function (both halves real and non-trivial, the two public halves distinct storage, the three derived seeds pairwise distinct, the composed widths matching each parameter set), bounded loops, named errors composed honestly from every rung (HkdfError, KeyGenError, MlkemSealedError, Bip39Error). Proven at all three tiers with no fabricated constant: the BIP-39 half anchored to a real published answer, no drift from the composed halves, the two keys distinct, a real working signing half, a sealing-ready agreement half, both halves at once, and determinism plus distinctness. The module's own functions call no std.crypto — each half is doubly-oracle-proven one rung down. Every identity is a TEST identity from a published-vector mnemonic — no key held, no network, no funds, no device; growing a real post-quantum identity from the maintainer's own phrase stays the custody gate, constant-time timing-safety the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/kumara_pq_identity"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/kumara_pq_identity.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/kumara_pq_identity.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-kumara-pq-identity' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-kumara-pq-identity: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-kumara-pq-identity line"
